    "answer": {
      "short": "Assuming $n$ is a positive integer, the 100-fold composition is\n$$F_{100}(x)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+100x^n}}.$$\n\nEach composition increases the coefficient of $x^n$ in the denominator's radicand by $1$.\n\nRetain the original iteration domain: $\\mathbb R$ for even $n$; $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-j^{-1/n}:j=1,\\ldots,100\\}$ for odd $n$.",
      "worked": "Assume $n$ is a positive integer; this is an editorial assumption because the source does not specify its permitted values. Let $F_k$ denote the $k$-fold composition of $f$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}F_2(x)&=\\frac{\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+x^n}}}{\\sqrt[n]{1+\\frac{x^n}{1+x^n}}}\\\\F_2(x)&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+2x^n}}\\\\F_3(x)&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+3x^n}}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe pattern is $F_k(x)=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+kx^n}}$. Substituting this expression into $f$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}F_{k+1}(x)&=f(F_k(x))\\\\F_{k+1}(x)&=\\frac{\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+kx^n}}}{\\sqrt[n]{1+\\frac{x^n}{1+kx^n}}}\\\\F_{k+1}(x)&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+(k+1)x^n}}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe formula holds for $k=1$, and each further composition increases $k$ by $1$. Hence\n\n$$F_{100}(x)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt[n]{1+100x^n}}.$$\n\nAll simplifications retain the restrictions from earlier applications. For even $n$, every expression $1+jx^n$ is positive, so all 100 applications are defined for every real $x$.\n\nFor odd $n$, negative radicands are permitted, but each denominator must be nonzero. At application $j$, taking $F_0(x)=x$ and assuming the preceding applications exist,\n\n$$1+F_{j-1}(x)^n=\\frac{1+jx^n}{1+(j-1)x^n}.$$\n\nThus application $j$ also excludes $x=-j^{-1/n}$. The domain of the original composition is\n\n$$D_{100}=\\begin{cases}\\mathbb R,&n\\text{ even},\\\\\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-j^{-1/n}:j=1,\\ldots,100\\},&n\\text{ odd}.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe simplified final expression does not restore inputs excluded by any earlier application.",
